= Parkinson's law — Technical =

== Primary-source interpretation ==
C. Northcote Parkinson's essay, first published in ''The Economist'' on 19 November 1955 and later collected in book form, combines an opening observation about allotted time with a longer satire of British public administration.<ref name="parkinson">Parkinson, C. Northcote. “Parkinson's Law,” ''The Economist'', 19 November 1955; later reprint “Parkinson's Law or The Rising Pyramid” (1958), https://www.panarchy.org/parkinson/parkinsonlaw.html . This is a reproduction, not a critical scholarly edition.</ref> Its joke depends partly on treating selected administrative time series and a formal-looking personnel equation as though they established a universal law. The historical data are illustrative observations, not a random sample of bureaucracies.

== Two distinct claims ==
=== Time-allocation hypothesis ===
For a fixed deliverable, time actually spent may depend on the allowed deadline, even after accounting for complexity. This is a '''hypothesis''', not the identity <math>T_{actual}=T_{allowed}</math>. An empirical test would randomize feasible deadlines for comparable tasks, predefine minimum quality and safety requirements, and measure effort, completion, revisions and outcome quality. Some work has irreducible physical or regulatory time constraints; changing the deadline may instead change the deliverable.

=== Administrative-growth hypothesis ===
Parkinson described an incentive to appoint subordinates rather than rivals and a feedback process in which officials generate work for each other. One can operationalize this as a hypothesis that administrative staff growth is partly independent of measured external service demand. The alternative is that administrative functions legitimately expand with complexity, accountability, or regulatory obligations.

== Historical comparisons and inference ==
The original essay contrasted declining naval measures with increasing administrative staffing, and discussed British colonial administration.<ref name="parkinson"/> Such comparisons require a stable definition of staff and workload. Fleet size is not a sufficient measure of all administrative work: technological change, postwar restructuring, procurement, policy and accounting practices may matter. A rising numerator with a falling proxy denominator does not by itself establish wasted effort or the proposed hiring mechanism.

A careful observational analysis would collect multiple agencies over time, distinguish administrative from operational positions, adjust for changing responsibilities, and investigate policy discontinuities. Even then, correlations cannot uniquely identify motives. Breton and Wintrobe subsequently examined the bureaucratic-growth argument as a question in political economy, illustrating that the claim is a research problem rather than a settled deterministic law.<ref name="breton">Breton, Albert, and Ronald Wintrobe. “Bureaucracy and state intervention: Parkinson's Law?” Publisher-hosted record (1979): https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1754-7121.1979.tb01813.x .</ref>

== Model identification and limitations ==
A possible descriptive model of staffing is <math>N_{t+1}=N_t+H_t-E_t</math>, where <math>H_t</math> and <math>E_t</math> count hires and exits in the same interval. This is merely an accounting identity, '''not Parkinson's formula'''. To test his mechanism, one must independently measure supervisory incentives, internally generated coordination tasks, external demand and organizational changes. No universal numerical rate is asserted here.

For time allocation, a deadline manipulation is informative only if task specification, resources, participant incentives and output-quality measurement are controlled. Evidence for one kind of knowledge work would not imply the same behavior in emergency medicine, manufacturing, or safety-critical engineering.

== Interpretation ==
The law remains useful as a prompt to examine deadline-induced elaboration and self-reinforcing administration. Its rhetorical effectiveness does not substitute for causal evidence, and neither the time aphorism nor the personnel satire should be presented as a quantitative prediction.

